\ Module A defines its own `probe`; the entry imports a colliding `probe` from
\ module B. A's stored quotation runs interpreted inside the image, and its
\ bare `probe` is baked to A's word row at build time, so it resolves without
\ a name lookup and cannot land on B's export.
\
\ Code created after freeze has no baked target and must still resolve by
\ name, through the entry file's own serialized import set: the `eval-string`
\ below sees B's `probe`, exactly as the entry body does.

use { a-quot: } "fixtures/own_module_image_resolved_a.1z" ;
use { probe: } "fixtures/own_module_image_resolved_b.1z" ;

probe print-line
a-quot call print-line
"probe print-line" eval-string
